            "id": "m03",
            "title": "Adi Shankaracharya (8th Century): Advaita Vedanta, Maya & The Four Mathas",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Adi Shankaracharya: Advaita Vedanta, Maya & The Four Mathas</h3>\n            <p>Adi Shankaracharya (c. 788–820 AD) was one of the most brilliant philosophers and intellectual synthesizers in world history. In a short lifespan of only 32 years, he revived Vedic Hinduism, unified diverse philosophical traditions, and propounded the doctrine of <strong>Advaita Vedanta (Absolute Non-Dualism)</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Early Life and Renunciation</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Born in the village of <strong>Kaladi</strong> on the banks of the Periyar River (in modern Kerala) to a Nambudiri Brahmin couple, Shivaguru and Aryamba.</li>\n                <li>Displaying prodigious intellectual genius, he mastered the four Vedas by age eight, renounced worldly life, and traveled to the banks of the Narmada to become the disciple of <strong>Govinda Bhagavatpada</strong> (disciple of Gaudapada, the author of the <em>Mandukya Karika</em>).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Philosophy of Advaita Vedanta (Absolute Monism)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Core Axiom:</strong> Shankaracharya summarized his philosophy in the immortal aphorism:\n                    <br><em>\"Brahma Satyam Jagan Mithya, Jivo Brahmaiva Na Aparah\"</em>\n                    <br>(<em>\"Brahman [the Supreme Absolute Consciousness] alone is Real; the empirical world is Mithya [illusory/relative]; and the individual soul [Jiva] is none other than Brahman.\"</em>)\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Nirguna Brahman:</strong> Ultimate Reality is pure, undifferentiated, attributeless consciousness (<em>Nirguna, Nirakara</em>), transcending all duality, categories, and limitations.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Concept of Maya and Vivartavada:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>The appearance of a diversified material universe arises through the cosmic illusion of <strong>Maya</strong> (inscrutable power) acting via <strong>Avidya</strong> (individual ignorance).</li>\n                        <li><em>Vivartavada (Doctrine of Superimposition / Apparent Transformation):</em> Just as in dim light, a person mistakenly superimposes a snake onto a piece of rope (<em>Rajjusarpa Nyaya</em>), causing genuine fear until the true rope is illuminated, similarly, empirical souls superimpose the material world of names and forms upon the unchanging substratum of Brahman.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Three Orders of Reality (Satta-Traividhya):</strong>\n                    <ol>\n                        <li><strong>Paramarthika Satta (Absolute Reality):</strong> Nirguna Brahman, eternal, unchanging, and non-dual.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Vyavaharika Satta (Empirical / Pragmatic Reality):</strong> The physical everyday world experienced by the senses, governed by physical and moral laws until spiritual enlightenment.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Pratibhasika Satta (Apparent / Illusory Reality):</strong> Subjective illusions, dreams, and optical mirages.</li>\n                    </ol>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Major Commentaries (Prasthanatrayi Bhashyas)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Shankara composed monumental commentaries (<em>Bhashyas</em>) on the triple foundation of Vedanta—the <strong>Prasthanatrayi</strong>:\n                    <ol>\n                        <li>The Principal <strong>Upanishads</strong> (Brihadaranyaka, Chandogya, Taittiriya, Katha, Isha, etc.).</li>\n                        <li>The <strong>Bhagavad Gita</strong>.</li>\n                        <li>Badarayana’s <strong>Brahma Sutras</strong> (his crowning philosophical work).</li>\n                    </ol>\n                </li>\n                <li>He also composed timeless devotional and philosophical hymns, including <em>Vivekachudamani</em>, <em>Upadeshasahasri</em>, <em>Atma Bodha</em>, and the famous devotional poem <strong>Bhaja Govindam (Moha Mudgara)</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. Monastic Organization: The Four Cardinal Mathas</h4>\n            <p>To preserve Vedic philosophy and maintain spiritual unity across the four corners of India, Shankara established four supreme monastic seats (<em>Amnaya Mathas</em>), each associated with one of the four Vedas and headed by a designated disciple:</p>\n            <table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"6\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; margin-top: 10px;\">\n                <tr style=\"background-color: #f0f0f0;\">\n                    <th>Cardinal Direction</th>\n                    <th>Monastic Matha</th>\n                    <th>Location</th>\n                    <th>Associated Veda</th>\n                    <th>First Acharya</th>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>North</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Jyotirmath</strong> (Badrikashram)</td>\n                    <td>Uttarakhand</td>\n                    <td>Atharva Veda</td>\n                    <td>Totakacharya</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>South</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Sringeri Sharada Peetham</strong></td>\n                    <td>Karnataka</td>\n                    <td>Yajur Veda</td>\n                    <td>Sureshwaracharya</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>East</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Govardhana Matha</strong></td>\n                    <td>Puri (Odisha)</td>\n                    <td>Rig Veda</td>\n                    <td>Padmapadacharya</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>West</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Sharada Peetham (Dwarka)</strong></td>\n                    <td>Gujarat</td>\n                    <td>Sama Veda</td>\n                    <td>Hastamalakacharya</td>\n                </tr>\n            </table>\n            <p>He also organized Hindu sannyasis into ten monastic orders (<strong>Dashanami Sampradaya</strong>: Giri, Puri, Bharati, Aranya, Tirtha, Ashrama, Vana, Parvata, Sagara, and Saraswati).</p>\n        ",
