            "id": "m04",
            "title": "Ramanujacharya (11th Century): Vishishtadvaita, Saguna Brahman & Prapatti",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Ramanujacharya: Vishishtadvaita, Saguna Brahman & Prapatti</h3>\n            <p>Sri Ramanujacharya (c. 1017–1137 AD) was the supreme philosophical architect of South Indian <strong>Sri Vaishnavism</strong>. Rejecting Shankaracharya’s concept of the world as cosmic illusion (Maya), Ramanuja constructed an intellectually rigorous theistic philosophy that harmonized Vedic philosophy with passionate temple devotion.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Early Life and the Breach with Monism</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Born at <strong>Sriperumbudur</strong> (near modern Chennai, Tamil Nadu) in 1017 AD to Kesava Somayaji and Kantimathi.</li>\n                <li>Initially studied Advaita Vedanta at Kanchipuram under <strong>Yadavaprakasha</strong>. However, Ramanuja revolted against Yadavaprakasha’s interpretation of Brahman as impersonal and devoid of qualities, famously defending the divine form and compassionate beauty of God.</li>\n                <li>He traveled to <strong>Srirangam</strong> to become the successor of the great Vaishnava Acharya <strong>Yamunacharya</strong> (Alavandar), assuming the pontificate of the prestigious Ranganathaswamy Temple.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Philosophy of Vishishtadvaita (Qualified Non-Dualism)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Meaning of Vishishtadvaita:</strong> <em>Vishishta-Advaita</em> signifies 'Non-dualism of the Qualified Whole'. Reality is organic and unified, but contains real, eternal internal distinctions.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Triad of Real Entities (Tattva-Traya):</strong> Ramanuja posited three eternally real realities:\n                    <ol>\n                        <li><strong>Ishvara (God / Narayana / Vishnu):</strong> The Supreme Lord, possessing infinite auspicious qualities (<em>Ananta Kalyana Guna</em>), such as omniscience, omnipotence, and boundless compassion.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Cit (Individual Souls / Jivas):</strong> Conscious, sentient beings who are eternally distinct from God, yet dependent upon Him.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Acit (Insentient Matter / Prakriti):</strong> The physical universe, real and continuously evolving.</li>\n                    </ol>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>The Body-Soul Analogy (Sharira-Shariri Bhava):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Ramanuja explained the relationship between God and the universe using the organic metaphor of the <strong>Body and the Soul</strong>:\n                            <br>Just as the physical body is completely dependent on, controlled by, and exists for the sake of the residing soul, similarly, <strong>all souls and the entire material universe constitute the 'body' of God (Sharira)</strong>, while Narayana is the supreme cosmic indwelling Soul (<em>Shariri</em> or <em>Antaryamin</em>).\n                        </li>\n                        <li>Therefore, the material world is <strong>not an illusion (Maya)</strong>; it is completely real, holy, and a manifestation of God's glory.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. The Path of Salvation: Bhakti and Prapatti (Sharanagati)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Rejection of Intellectual Jnana Alone:</strong> Ramanuja argued that dry intellectual knowledge cannot liberate. Salvation requires continuous, loving meditation on God (<em>Bhakti</em>).</li>\n                <li><strong>Prapatti (Absolute Self-Surrender):</strong> Recognizing that rigorous Vedic yoga and ritual meditation were inaccessible to ordinary mortals, women, and low-caste outcasts, Ramanuja championed <strong>Prapatti</strong> (or <em>Sharanagati</em>)—complete, unreserved, and humble self-surrender to the divine mercy of Narayana. Prapatti was declared universally open to all human beings without distinction of caste, creed, or gender.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. Monumental Social Reforms & Literary Works</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Gospel from the Gopuram:</strong> When his guru <strong>Gosthipurna (Nambi)</strong> initiated him into the sacred eight-syllable Ashtakshari mantra (<em>'Om Namo Narayanaya'</em>) under strict oath of secrecy upon pain of hell, Ramanuja immediately climbed to the top of the towering temple gopuram at <strong>Thirukoshtiyur</strong> and shouted the liberating mantra to all assembled villagers, proclaiming: <em>\"If my disobedience sends me alone to hell, but saves thousands of my fellow human beings, I gladly accept hell!\"</em></li>\n                <li><strong>Temple Entry for Dalits:</strong> He introduced outcastes into temple worship, naming them <strong>Thirukulattar</strong> ('People of the Noble Clan') and granting them entry into the Melkote Cheluvanarayana Swamy Temple during annual festivals.</li>\n                <li><strong>Master Literature:</strong> Authored the <strong>Sri Bhashya</strong> (his monumental commentary on the Brahma Sutras refuting Shankara's Advaita), <em>Gita Bhashya</em>, and <em>Vedanta Dipa</em>.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
