            "id": "m08",
            "title": "Guru Nanak Dev (1469–1539 AD): Ik Onkar, Langar, Pangat & Sangat",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Guru Nanak Dev: Ik Onkar, Langar, Pangat & Sangat</h3>\n            <p>Guru Nanak Dev (1469–1539 AD) was the founder of Sikhism and one of the most sublime spiritual luminaries in world history. Rejecting ascetic renunciation, idolatry, and caste stratification, he envisioned a dynamic, egalitarian society based on active moral duty, honest labor, and the remembrance of the Divine Name.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Early Life and Spiritual Awakening</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Birth at Nankana Sahib:</strong> Born on April 15, 1469 at <strong>Rai Bhoi di Talwandi</strong> (modern Nankana Sahib, Punjab, Pakistan) to Mehta Kalu (a Bedi Khatri patwari / village accountant) and Mata Tripta.</li>\n                <li><strong>Sacha Sauda (The True Bargain):</strong> As a youth, his father gave him twenty rupees to conduct a profitable business trade; instead, Nanak used the entire sum to purchase food and feed starving ascetics and wandering sadhus, declaring that feeding the hungry was the only <em>'True Bargain'</em>.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Enlightenment at Kali Bein (c. 1499 AD):</strong> While employed as the storekeeper (<em>Modi</em>) of the state granary at Sultanpur Lodhi, Nanak went for his morning ablution in the <strong>Kali Bein River</strong> and vanished for three days. When he reappeared, suffused with divine illumination, his first immortal proclamation was:\n                    <br><em>\"Na koi Hindu, na koi Musalman\"</em>\n                    <br>(<em>\"There is no Hindu, there is no Muslim\"</em>—reminding humanity that in the eyes of the Creator, all labels of institutional religion are meaningless compared to divine virtue).\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Core Metaphysical and Ethical Pillars</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Ik Onkar (The One Indivisible Reality):</strong> Sikh scripture opens with the sacred glyph <strong>'ੴ' (Ik Onkar)</strong>, declaring the absolute oneness, omnipresence, and timeless transcendence of God (<em>Akal Purakh</em>), who is uncreated, fearless (<em>Nirbhau</em>), and devoid of malice (<em>Nirvair</em>).</li>\n                <li><strong>The Three Core Ethical Commandments:</strong>\n                    <ol>\n                        <li><strong>Nam Japna:</strong> Continuous, meditative contemplation of the Divine Name (<em>Satnam</em>) to conquer the five inner vices (ego, lust, anger, greed, attachment).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Kirat Karo:</strong> Earning one’s livelihood through honest, hard, and ethical labor. Nanak radically rejected ascetic begging, parasitism, and hermitage retreat, elevating the life of the working householder (<em>Grihastha</em>).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Vand Chhako:</strong> Sharing one’s earnings with the needy, impoverished, and community before partaking oneself.</li>\n                    </ol>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Institutional Innovations: Langar, Pangat & Sangat</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>To translate theological equality into everyday social reality, Guru Nanak established three revolutionary egalitarian institutions:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Sangat (Holy Congregation):</strong> A democratic assembly where men and women of all social classes, castes, and faiths gathered on equal terms to sing divine praises (<em>Kirtan</em>).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Langar (The Free Community Kitchen):</strong> A revolutionary communal meal funded by voluntary community contributions, prepared and served by devotees regardless of social status.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Pangat (Eating in a Straight Row):</strong> An uncompromising social equalizer: the emperor and the outcaste, the Brahmin and the Dalit, were required to sit side-by-side on the bare floor in a straight line (<em>Pangat</em>) to partake of the same vegetarian food before entering the presence of the Guru. This demolished the centuries-old taboo of ritual pollution through inter-dining.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. The Udasis and Successorship</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Four Udasis (Spiritual Journeys):</strong> Guru Nanak undertook four epic journeys spanning nearly 30,000 km across Asia, accompanied by his lifelong companion, the Muslim minstrel <strong>Bhai Mardana</strong>, who played the plucked string instrument, the <em>Rabab</em>. He visited holy centers across India, Sri Lanka, Tibet, Mecca, Medina, and Baghdad, debating with pandits, qazis, and yogis.</li>\n                <li><strong>Settlement at Kartarpur:</strong> In 1522, he settled at <strong>Kartarpur</strong> on the banks of the Ravi River, living as a farmer and establishing the first Sikh commune.</li>\n                <li><strong>Appointment of Guru Angad:</strong> Passing over his own sons (Sri Chand, who founded the ascetic Udasi sect, and Lakhmi Das), Nanak selected his most devoted and obedient disciple, <strong>Bhai Lehna</strong>, renaming him <strong>Guru Angad Dev</strong> ('A limb of my own body'), institutionalizing a continuous line of spiritual gurus.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
