            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Female Mystics & Saguna Devotion: Mirabai, Lal Ded & Akka Mahadevi</h3>\n            <p>The medieval Bhakti movement was revolutionary in its empowerment of women. Female mystics shattered patriarchal oppression, defied royal courts and forced marriages, and expressed sublime spiritual sovereignty through vernacular poetry.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Mirabai (1498–1546 AD): The Queen of Krishna Devotion</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Royal Lineage:</strong> Born around 1498 at Kudki in the Merta district of Rajasthan, Mirabai was the daughter of Ratan Singh Rathore (descendant of Rao Jodha, founder of Jodhpur).</li>\n                <li><strong>Marriage to Mewar:</strong> In 1516, she was married for dynastic reasons to <strong>Bhojraj</strong>, the eldest son and heir-apparent of the legendary warrior <strong>Rana Sanga of Mewar</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Refusal of Sati and Divine Bridehood:</strong> When Prince Bhojraj died in battle in 1521, Mirabai adamantly refused to commit <em>Sati</em> on his funeral pyre. She proclaimed that she was already married to the immortal Lord:\n                    <br><em>\"Mere to Giridhar Gopal, dusro na koi; Jake sir mor-mukut, mero pati soi\"</em>\n                    <br>(<em>\"Mine is only Giridhar Gopal [Krishna], none other; He who wears the peacock feather upon His crown, He alone is my true husband.\"</em>)\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Persecution by the Court:</strong> Following Rana Sanga’s death, the new ruler, <strong>Rana Vikramaditya</strong>, subjected Mira to cruel harassment for mingling freely with holy sadhus and dancing in temples.\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>The Rana sent her a cup filled with deadly poison (<em>Visha ka Pyala</em>), claiming it was charanamrita; Mira drank it with joyous surrender, and by Krishna’s grace, it turned into sweet nectar without harming her.</li>\n                        <li>He sent her a wicker basket concealing a venomous black cobra; when Mira opened it, it had miraculously turned into a fragrant garland of flowers and a Shaligram stone.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Wandering and Death at Dwarka:</strong> Leaving the palaces of Chittor, Mira wandered as a singing mendicant through Merta and Vrindavan, finally settling at the <strong>Ranikhet / Dwarkadhish Temple in Dwarka</strong> (Gujarat). In 1546, legend recounts that while singing before the deity, she was miraculously absorbed into the stone idol of Krishna. Her passionate <em>Padas</em> and <em>Bhajans</em> in Rajasthani Brajbhasha remain immortal treasures of Indian music.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Lal Ded / Lalleshwari (14th Century, Kashmir)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Life:</strong> Born to a Kashmiri Pandit family at Pandrethan near Srinagar, she was married into a cruel household where her mother-in-law starved and tormented her. At age twenty-four, she renounced domestic life to wander as a Shaivite yogini.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Vakhs:</strong> Lal Ded composed her mystical realizations in four-line poetic stanzas known as <strong>Vakhs</strong> (literally 'sayings' or 'speech') in the old Kashmiri language, representing the fountainhead of Kashmiri literature.</li>\n                <li><strong>Philosophy of Trika Shaivism:</strong> She taught that Shiva is the non-dual inner light dwelling within the cave of the human heart, mocking external asceticism, temple rituals, and animal slaughter:\n                    <br><em>\"The idol is but stone, the temple is but stone; from top to bottom, all is one stone! O foolish priest, whom do you worship? Unify your mind with the Living Spirit!\"</em>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Syncretic Veneration:</strong> Revered equally by Kashmiri Hindus (who call her <em>Lalleshwari</em> or <em>Lal Ded</em>) and Muslims (who call her <em>Lalla Arifa</em>), she profoundly influenced <strong>Sheikh Nuruddin Wali (Nund Rishi)</strong>, the founder of the syncretic Kashmiri <em>Rishi order</em>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Akka Mahadevi (12th Century, Karnataka)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Virashaiva Radical:</strong> Born at Udutadi (Shimoga district, Karnataka), Akka Mahadevi was a prominent female mystic of the <strong>Virashaiva (Lingayat)</strong> movement led by Basaveshwara.</li>\n                <li><strong>Chennamallikarjuna:</strong> She considered Lord Shiva in His form as <strong>Chennamallikarjuna</strong> ('The Lord White as Jasmine') as her eternal celestial bridegroom, refusing to consummate her forced marriage to the local Jain king Kaushika.</li>\n                <li><strong>Shedding Social Modesty:</strong> In a radical act of spiritual defiance against patriarchal norms, Akka Mahadevi discarded all clothes and social modesty, wandering completely naked with only her long tresses covering her body.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Vachanas:</strong> She composed over 430 sublime, passionate free-verse Kannada spiritual poems known as <strong>Vachanas</strong>, ending each with the signature invocation to <em>Chennamallikarjuna</em>. She participated in the philosophical assembly of the <strong>Anubhava Mantapa</strong> in Kalyana alongside Allama Prabhu, before merging into the divine at Srisailam.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
