            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Medieval Hindu Jurisprudence: The Mitakshara vs Dayabhaga</h3>\n            <p>During the 11th and 12th centuries, classical Hindu civil and family law (<em>Vyavahara</em>) was crystallized through master legal commentaries on the ancient Dharmashastras. Two great rival schools of jurisprudence emerged—the <strong>Mitakshara School</strong> and the <strong>Dayabhaga School</strong>—which governed Hindu inheritance, property rights, and joint-family succession until modern times.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Mitakshara School of Vijnaneshvara (11th Century AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Author and Era:</strong> Composed by <strong>Vijnaneshvara</strong> (c. 1076–1127 AD), the celebrated court scholar and jurist of the Western Chalukya emperor <strong>Vikramaditya VI</strong> of Kalyana (Karnataka).</li>\n                <li><strong>The Text:</strong> The <em>Mitakshara</em> is an exhaustive legal commentary on the ancient <strong>Yajnavalkya Smriti</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Core Legal Principles of Inheritance:</strong>\n                    <ol>\n                        <li><strong>Inalienable Right by Birth (Janmasvatva):</strong> A son, grandson, and great-grandson acquire an immediate, vested legal <strong>right in ancestral family property from the very moment of their birth</strong>.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Doctrine of Survivorship (Apratibandha Daya):</strong> Ancestral property is held jointly by a male <em>Coparcenary</em>. Ownership does not pass upon the death of the father, but continuously fluctuates with births and deaths, surviving in the remaining male coparceners.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Right to Demand Partition:</strong> Because a son is a co-owner by birth, he possesses the absolute legal right to <strong>demand partition of ancestral property from his father</strong> even during the father’s lifetime.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Limited Alienation:</strong> The father cannot gift, sell, or mortgage ancestral immovable property without the consent of his coparcener sons, except during acute distress (<em>Apatkala</em>) or for pious family necessity.</li>\n                    </ol>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Geographical Jurisdiction:</strong> The Mitakshara school held absolute legal sway throughout the <strong>entire Indian subcontinent EXCEPT Bengal and Assam</strong>, branching into four sub-schools: Benares, Mithila, Maharashtra (Bombay), and Dravida (Madras).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Dayabhaga School of Jimutavahana (12th Century AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Author and Era:</strong> Authored by <strong>Jimutavahana</strong> during the 12th century under the Sena dynasty of Bengal, as part of his legal digest <em>Dharmaratna</em>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Etymology:</strong> <em>Daya-Bhaga</em> literally signifies 'Division or Partition of Heritage'.</li>\n                <li><strong>Core Legal Principles of Inheritance:</strong>\n                    <ol>\n                        <li><strong>No Right by Birth (Uparama-Svatva):</strong> A son acquires <strong>NO legal right whatsoever in ancestral property by birth</strong>. Property rights arise only upon the death of the ancestor (*Sapratibandha Daya*).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Absolute Authority of the Father:</strong> As long as the father is alive, he is the sole, absolute, and unfettered master of all family property (both ancestral and self-acquired). The father can sell, mortgage, or gift ancestral land without the consent of his sons.</li>\n                        <li><strong>No Right to Demand Partition:</strong> Sons have no legal power to demand partition while the father lives. Partition can take place only after the father's demise.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Doctrine of Religious Efficacy (Pindadana):</strong> The right to inherit property is determined strictly by the religious capacity of the heir to confer spiritual benefit upon the deceased ancestor by offering funeral pinda cakes.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Enhanced Rights for Widows:</strong> If a male coparcener dies childless, his surviving widow inherits his undivided share of the family property as a limited estate (<em>Stridhan</em>), a progressive right completely denied to childless widows under the strict Mitakshara system.</li>\n                    </ol>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Geographical Jurisdiction:</strong> Prevailed exclusively in <strong>Bengal and Assam</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Hemadri’s Chaturvarga Chintamani (13th Century AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Authored by <strong>Hemadri (Hemadpant)</strong>, the Chief Minister (<em>Karanadhipa</em>) of the Yadava rulers Mahadeva and Ramachandra of Devagiri.</li>\n                <li>A monumental Sanskrit legal and ritual encyclopedia in five sections (<em>Vratakhanda</em>, <em>Danakhanda</em>, <em>Tirthakhanda</em>, <em>Mokshakhanda</em>, and <em>Parishesakhanda</em>) codifying fasts, religious vows, charitable gifts, and pilgrimage rites. Hemadpant is also remembered in Maharashtra for introducing the Modi script for administrative cursive Marathi and the distinctive star-shaped Hemadpanthi temple architecture.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
